rust-redismultiplexer (1.1.0-1) stable; urgency=medium

  Speed
  * Without ordering, each child delivers its own packages: no queuer thread
    and no channel hops. Faster and less CPU per package (bench/BASELINE.md)
  * Packages are bytes from end to end: payloads that are not UTF-8 are
    delivered (1.0.8 lost them); filters are compiled once at start
  * With ordering, one sender delivers for every child, in strict ts order
    with any number of children (1.0.8: only with one child), writing up to
    1000 RPUSH (4 MiB) per round trip to each target
  * Stop within about a second without ordering (1.0.8: 2 to 4 s); with
    ordering, the time to deliver what the buffer holds, at the pipelined
    rate, with two lines: "Closing: delivering N held packages in order"
    and "Flushed N packages in T s"

  New optional keys (root and per client); files without them work as before
  * `database`, `username` (Redis 6 ACL); passwords are passed as written,
    so `#`, `/`, `?` and `%` work
  * `connect_timeout` (default 2 s) and `command_timeout` (default 5 s), in
    seconds, decimals allowed, at least 0.001; 0 waits as 1.0.8 did
  * `filter_until_included`: the `filter_until` delimiter belongs to the
    header (default false, as before)
  * `filter_replace_header`: replace the whole header with a literal text
  * Keys 1.0.8 ignored now act: a file that already had `database: 1`
    now delivers to database 1
  * `hostname` takes an IPv6 address written `::1` or `[::1]`

  Failures
  * A target that cannot be reached, closes or does not answer within
    `command_timeout` is down: the others keep receiving, it is connected
    again in the background after 1 s and then every 5 s at most, with one
    line when it goes down and one when it is back. Packages are not kept
    for it: replicant counts them failed for it; spreader gives them to the
    next target, so after an unanswered RPUSH a package can be on two
    targets (with ordering, up to one chunk)
  * The source has the same timeouts: a source silent for more than 6 s is
    connected again, and a reply that comes later than that loses its
    package; `command_timeout: 0` at the root restores the 1.0.8 wait
  * At its start each thread waits for its targets' first connection, up to
    their connect plus command timeout, before it reads the source
  * While the source is idle each connected target gets a PING once a
    second instead of an LLEN, so a target restarted at night is connected
    again before the next packages; an error reply to it keeps the link.
    A target that stops answering after its handshake holds that thread
    (with ordering, the sender) up to its `command_timeout` once per
    reconnection, and with ordering a package due meanwhile waits that long
  * Every connection is named with CLIENT SETNAME; no CLIENT SETINFO
  * With ordering, a target whose replies fail prints one error line per
    flush, not one per package

  Spreader with ordering
  * Packages are shared as the round robin shares them one by one. When a
    target refuses in its replies (an error reply, no reply, its connection
    lost while a chunk is written), what it refused goes to the next target
    that takes it: the shares of that chunk can differ from 1.0.8's by up to
    those packages, and the order
    at a target can break once per failure event, by at most the packages
    refused in that chunk; with client filters that depend on the package's
    place in the stream, the split after such an event can settle in
    another of the splits 1.0.8 itself gives. A target that comes back is
    used from the next chunk
  * A target that answers errors to every package is offered one package
    per chunk until it takes one, and is back to its share one chunk later
  * Replicant with ordering: a target that breaks in the middle of a chunk
    can miss the rest of that chunk (counted failed for it)

  Limits
  * `hardlimit` alone turns the limits on, with `softlimit` 80 % of it,
    `checklimit` 10000 and `timelimit` 1 s unless given; between two checks
    a queue can pass `hardlimit` by up to children x `checklimit` (one x
    `checklimit` with ordering)
  * A limit key at 0 is not set: a target with all four at 0 delivers
    (1.0.8: stuck for the whole run)
  * `deleteblock: 0` is read as not set, with a warning (1.0.8: an endless
    trim once over `hardlimit`); trims go to the end of the list, lists
    longer than 100000000 are no longer cut, and `deleted` counts what was
    removed
  * `timelimit: N` checks N s after the last check, on a monotonic clock
  * No LLEN for a target without limits; a stuck target is checked again
    once a second while the source is idle

  Ordering
  * A package is held `ordering_buffer_time` seconds from its arrival, on a
    monotonic clock in milliseconds (1.0.8: between T and T + 1 s); equal
    ts leave in arrival order (1.0.8: by payload bytes)
  * `ordering_buffer_time: 0` is read as 1 s, with a warning
  * The buffer is in memory and nothing holds the children back: with a
    target slower than the source it grows faster than with 1.0.8, for as
    long as that lasts, and the stop takes the time to deliver it
  * No combination of `ordering`, `ordering_buffer_time` and
    `ordering_limit` is an error any more; the odd ones get a warning
  * "without ordering information" and "couldn't be parsed" warnings are
    printed the first time, then at most once every 10 s per thread, with
    the count of packages since the line before

  Process
  * SIGTERM and SIGHUP stop cleanly, as SIGINT: the ordering buffer is
    delivered and the pid file removed (1.0.8: killed). Started under
    nohup, SIGHUP stays ignored
  * Exit codes: 0 after a stop by a signal, `--version` or `--help`; 2 for
    a usage or configuration error (no argument, a file missing or
    unreadable, YAML, validation, an unwritable pid file); 1 when a child
    or the sender ended on its own (1.0.8: 0 for all)
  * A child or the sender that ends on its own stops the program, with the
    final status written, the pid file removed and what the sender held
    counted as dropped
  * Warnings at start, the program still starts: an unknown key (by name),
    an empty or repeated client name, `softlimit` not under `hardlimit`,
    `deleteblock: 0`, and the ordering cases above
  * The banner and `--help` show the version without a build date

  Status file
  * Every 1.0.8 key keeps its meaning. Added: `filtered`, `stuck`,
    `failed` (the breakdown of `drop`, as rates and `total_*` counts),
    `queue`, `stuck_targets`, `down_targets`, `since_start` (counts since
    the start) and `version`
  * Written to `<status>.tmp` and renamed, so a reader never sees half a
    file; where that cannot be done, in place with one warning

  Log
  * Colours only when the stream is a terminal: log files and the journal
    have no colour codes
  * Each line is written whole in one call: lines of different threads are
    no longer mixed
  * A line that cannot be written (a closed pipe, a full disk) is dropped
    and the program goes on (1.0.8: the thread panicked)

  Reading the configuration (serde_norway instead of serde_yaml 0.8)
  * An empty `password:` means no password (1.0.8 sent "~")
  * `True`, `TRUE`, `False`, `FALSE` are booleans; `Null`, `NULL` on an
    optional key mean the key is absent; CR-only line ends are accepted

  Files that 1.0.8 started and 1.1.0 refuses
  * Control characters in the file (U+0085, U+2028 and U+2029 that end a
    value are line breaks instead, and cut the value there); a value at
    column 0 on the line after its key; an empty `hostname:` or `mode:`
    (never worked)
  * A client `filter` that does not compile (1.0.8 started and its children
    panicked)
  * A value of the wrong type on a key name 1.0.8 ignored: `database`,
    `username`, `connect_timeout`, `command_timeout`,
    `filter_until_included`, `filter_replace_header`; a timeout above 0 and
    below 0.001 s; `filter_until_included` or `filter_replace_header`
    without `filter`, or `filter_replace` with `filter_replace_header`

  Packaging
  * redis crate 0.32.7 with native TLS; build-time, dict and thread_tryjoin
    removed
  * The crate holds only what builds and tests the program
  * Snap on base core26

